1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:07,860
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,030
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,910
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,250
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:17,280
What exactly is a phrasal verb when we talk about business communication?

6
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:22,210
Okay, so the way they work is actually, well, deceptively simple.

7
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:25,870
It's just a main verb combined with one or more particles.

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:28,070
Particles, like prepositions or adverbs.

9
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:29,840
Exactly, prepositions or adverbs.

10
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:37,910
But the key thing, and this is where the complexity and the real value comes in, is that the combination creates this idiomatic meaning.

11
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:40,650
It's often completely different from the meaning of the individual words.

12
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:46,680
Let's kick things off with the real engine of business, taking action and managing workflow.

13
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,490
Okay, this first group, it's all about execution.

14
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:51,130
Pure execution.

15
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,130
This is the language of initiating things, taking responsibility, and keeping efforts going.

16
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:58,610
If you want to sound like someone who actually moves things forward.

17
00:00:58,650 --> 00:00:59,250
You need these.

18
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:00,720
You absolutely need these verbs.

19
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:01,170
All right.

20
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,380
Let's start with accepting and initiating tasks.

21
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:09,560
First one on our list, and this feels really relevant for anyone ambitious, is take on.

22
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:11,330
The source example is pretty clear.

23
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:14,930
She decided to take on the new project despite the tight deadline.

24
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:15,250
Yeah.

25
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:16,690
Notice that particle on there.

26
00:01:17,110 --> 00:01:22,170
It kind of implies adding something or accepting something that might be a bit heavy, something placed upon you.

27
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:22,540
Right.

28
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:30,160
And what's fascinating here is the professional difference between just accepting a project and taking on a project.

29
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:34,880
If you just accept it, well, it's neutral, maybe even a bit passive.

30
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:38,080
But if you take it on, that signals ownership.

31
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:45,400
It signals initiative, a willingness to really tackle a challenge, especially like in the example when the conditions are tough, like that tight deadline.

32
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:50,540
It's a really powerful signal of commitment to, say, your manager.

33
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:51,460
That makes a lot of sense.

34
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:53,170
It's conveying an attitude, isn't it?

35
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:54,510
Not just the action itself.

36
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:54,910
Exactly.

37
00:01:55,210 --> 00:01:55,690
Attitude.

38
00:01:55,950 --> 00:02:02,680
But OK, if you're the one organizing the structure for that project, you need maybe a more foundational verb, like establishing something.

39
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,480
That brings us to set up the example.

40
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,640
They set up a meeting to discuss project objectives.

41
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,780
And this one is incredibly versatile.

42
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:14,830
We often use it for logistical things like setting up a meeting room or even a physical location.

43
00:02:15,290 --> 00:02:15,390
Sure.

44
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:18,170
But in today's business world, you also set up processes.

45
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:19,250
You set up protocols.

46
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:21,220
You might set up a whole new digital system.

47
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:29,870
It really means preparing the groundwork, the infrastructure, making sure everything is arranged and established properly before the main action kicks off.

48
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:30,150
Right.

49
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:30,830
Laying the foundation.

50
00:02:30,910 --> 00:02:35,380
OK. Moving beyond just initiating, we have take up.

51
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:40,740
The example given is she decided to take up additional tasks to improve her skills and experience.

52
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:42,660
How is this different from take on?

53
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:44,030
They sound similar.

54
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:47,660
They do sound similar, but there's a subtle difference, often in the motivation.

55
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:53,510
While take-on often refers to, say, an assignment or a challenge that's kind of given to you.

56
00:02:53,670 --> 00:02:54,370
Right, assigned.

57
00:02:54,790 --> 00:03:01,640
Key take-up frequently relates more to starting something regularly, often voluntarily, maybe for self-improvement.

58
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:07,590
Like in the example, taking up extra tasks or maybe taking up a new methodology, learning a new skill.

59
00:03:08,230 --> 00:03:09,800
It implies a proactive choice.

60
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:10,700
Ah, okay.

61
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:12,240
More personal initiative.

62
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:16,350
Yeah, it does have that secondary meaning, too, about consuming time or space.

63
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:18,980
You know, this project will take up a lot of my time.

64
00:03:19,630 --> 00:03:23,580
But professionally, the TwiSense is often about starting a new practice or habit.

65
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:24,060
Got it.

66
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:26,440
Okay, so we've initiated the project.

67
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:27,780
We've accepted the challenge.

68
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:30,810
Now, how do we talk about persistence, sticking with it?

69
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,870
That brings us to maintaining and continuing.

70
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:35,770
And the classic phrase here is carry on.

71
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,400
Example, let's carry on with the presentation despite the technical difficulties.

72
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,210
Oh, that phrase is just, it's pure essential resilience, isn't it?

73
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:43,600
Yeah.

74
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,970
In any work environment, setbacks are, well, they're inevitable.

75
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:50,390
The server crashes, someone interrupts, there's a technical glitch.

76
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:51,580
Happens all the time.

77
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:52,180
All the time.

78
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:58,110
Carry on acknowledges that disruption, but absolutely insists on maintaining the original course of action.

79
00:03:58,810 --> 00:04:04,850
It feels much stronger than just saying continue because it inherently implies overcoming some kind of obstacle.

80
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:05,220
Yeah.

81
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,000
Pushing through.

82
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:12,150
And what if you're responsible for maintaining something specific, maybe just temporarily like covering for someone?

83
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:12,890
You look after it.

84
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:16,970
The example is she'll look after the client accounts while the manager is away.

85
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,120
Yeah, this is critical.

86
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:23,190
Look after means taking care of something or someone, ensuring their needs are met.

87
00:04:23,210 --> 00:04:28,570
Or in this business context, ensuring an asset like those client accounts is maintained responsibly.

88
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:29,200
Okay.

89
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:34,020
It's really the language of temporary stewardship, and it implies absolute trustworthiness.

90
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:41,740
If you're delegated a task using look after, your manager is essentially trusting you with some of their most valued assets or responsibilities.

91
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:42,560
Right.

92
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:43,840
Big responsibility.

93
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:47,610
Okay, let's switch slightly to execution and compliance.

94
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:48,570
Doing what you're supposed to do.

95
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:52,940
The most functional term for just performing a plan seems to be carry out.

96
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,970
Example, the team will carry out the project as outlined in the proposal.

97
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:59,840
Yeah, this is implementation at its, well, its clearest.

98
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:05,350
It means executing the task or the plan exactly as it was intended or specified.

99
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:07,800
It signals reliable, faithful action.

100
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:13,260
There's zero ambiguity about performance expectations when you promise to carry out an instruction.

101
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:14,120
So it's quite formal.

102
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:14,730
It can be.

103
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:22,280
If you were writing, say, a technical document or even a legal contract, this is probably the verb you choose over just do or perform.

104
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:24,960
It has that weight of formal execution.

105
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:31,900
OK. Now, in any team, especially, you know, high performing ones, you need compromise sometimes.

106
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:33,940
People need to agree to move forward.

107
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:37,230
That brings us to go along with the example.

108
00:05:37,770 --> 00:05:41,310
He decided to go along with the team's decision to change the strategy.

109
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:42,790
This phrase is fascinating, actually.

110
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:47,740
That particle along suggests moving sort of parallel to someone else.

111
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:48,260
Right.

112
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:51,000
Signifies agreeing or complying with a suggestion or a decision.

113
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,530
Maybe even if you had some internal reservations.

114
00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:55,810
It's really the language of collaborative compromise.

115
00:05:55,870 --> 00:05:56,900
But is there a risk?

116
00:05:57,060 --> 00:06:01,670
Could using go along with sound a bit too informal or maybe weak?

117
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:03,790
That's a really good question, thinking about the context.

118
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:07,180
In spoken meetings, it's often perfect.

119
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:10,840
It shows a kind of democratic spirit, willingness to build consensus.

120
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:19,180
But if you were writing a formal response to, say, a senior executive, you might choose something like concur with or agree to implement.

121
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:26,290
But in the actual dynamics of a team discussion, going along with a consensus is often what keeps the project moving forward.

122
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:27,800
That distinction is really key.

123
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:38,030
So, yeah, the language in this whole segment, starting with taking on a challenge all the way to going along with the final decision, it really maps out the entire lifecycle of a task, doesn't it?

124
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:40,930
From assignment to consensus and execution.

125
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:41,480
Exactly.

126
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:43,000
It's the language of getting things done.

127
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:51,690
So if segment one was all about the doing, segment two covers that essential layer of interaction, you know, meetings, presentations, how information flows.

128
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:54,990
It relies so heavily on these specific verbs.

129
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:55,280
Right.

130
00:06:55,750 --> 00:06:57,590
Let's jump into introducing information first.

131
00:06:57,970 --> 00:07:00,990
Starting a tricky conversation, maybe, it needs a specific touch.

132
00:07:01,210 --> 00:07:02,300
And that's where bring up comes in.

133
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:03,100
Example.

134
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:06,560
He brought up the sales figures during the meeting to highlight performance.

135
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:07,160
Yeah.

136
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:15,880
Bring up is kind of the polite, professional way to introduce a topic, especially if it's one that might need, you know, careful handling.

137
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:18,740
Like maybe those sales figures aren't great or there are budget issues.

138
00:07:19,140 --> 00:07:19,440
OK.

139
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:23,410
Using bring up respects the sort of formal setting of a meeting.

140
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:27,710
If you just mention the sales figures, it might sound a bit too casual, almost throwaway.

141
00:07:28,150 --> 00:07:32,690
But bring up gives it the necessary weight for a proper discussion.

142
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:34,600
Is there a danger, though?

143
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:37,240
Could it be perceived as maybe a bit aggressive?

144
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:42,370
Like if I bring up a colleague's mistake, does that feel different than if I, say, raise the issue?

145
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:44,610
That's a great point about the social nuance of the boundary.

146
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:47,430
Generally, bring up is pretty neutral.

147
00:07:47,750 --> 00:07:49,740
It focuses on the topic itself.

148
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:57,430
If you say you raise an issue, that can sound a bit more formal, potentially more adversarial, like you're formally lodging a complaint.

149
00:07:57,410 --> 00:08:04,660
Bring up tends to keep the focus more on just the subject matter that needs discussing, not necessarily launching a critique.

150
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:05,500
Got it.

151
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:10,870
OK, so once that information say a report is on the table, we need to examine it.

152
00:08:11,130 --> 00:08:11,310
Right.

153
00:08:11,570 --> 00:08:12,300
Scrutinize it.

154
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:13,320
We need to go over it.

155
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:16,560
Example, please go over the report before submitting it.

156
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:17,740
Notice that particle.

157
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:18,320
Over.

158
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:25,210
It implies careful examination, maybe even repeated inspection, looking over the top of it metaphorically.

159
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:27,540
This signals immediate scrutiny.

160
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,960
We aren't just glancing at the report.

161
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,340
We are reviewing it, examining it carefully.

162
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:38,550
Usually the goal is to ensure accuracy, completeness, maybe catch errors before it goes further up the chain.

163
00:08:39,170 --> 00:08:42,200
It's a core action for quality control for due diligence.

164
00:08:42,250 --> 00:08:42,750
Makes sense.

165
00:08:43,430 --> 00:08:50,160
And for making sure information actually cascades down the organizational chart or across teams, we rely on pass on.

166
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:53,040
Example, please pass on my message to the team about the meeting.

167
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:53,720
Simple.

168
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:55,220
Yeah, but absolutely vital.

169
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:58,930
This highlights your role as a reliable link in the communication chain.

170
00:08:59,190 --> 00:09:02,690
You're essentially acting as a conduit, relaying a message from one person or group to another.

171
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:11,100
So thinking back to your point about formality, if it was something really formal, like an update to a regulatory body, you probably wouldn't say pass on.

172
00:09:11,220 --> 00:09:11,940
Probably not.

173
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:17,250
In that very formal context, you'd likely use convey or transmit the information.

174
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:24,480
But for everyday office life, peer-to-peer communication, manager to team pass on is just seamless.

175
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,900
It's the natural, efficient verb.

176
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:27,480
Okay.

177
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:29,870
Let's tackle the admin side of things, scheduling.

178
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:33,710
This is where things can get confusing, especially for non-native speakers.

179
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:38,680
Two critical verbs for changes, put off and call off.

180
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:39,500
Oh, absolutely.

181
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,120
Getting these mixed up can cause real problems.

182
00:09:42,490 --> 00:09:43,850
The distinction is fundamental.

183
00:09:44,390 --> 00:09:46,670
Put off simply means to delay or postpone.

184
00:09:46,770 --> 00:09:47,610
Okay, delay.

185
00:09:47,710 --> 00:09:48,760
Likes the example.

186
00:09:48,780 --> 00:09:52,200
They decided to put off the meeting until next week due to scheduling conflicts.

187
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:54,810
The key thing is the event will still happen just later.

188
00:09:55,150 --> 00:10:00,180
The particle off here kind of suggests removing it from the immediate schedule but not deleting it entirely.

189
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:02,700
Whereas call off, that's total cancellation.

190
00:10:02,760 --> 00:10:03,520
Total cancellation.

191
00:10:03,580 --> 00:10:03,900
Gone.

192
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,490
They called off the meeting due to scheduling conflicts with key participants.

193
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:09,130
The event is wiped clean from the calendar.

194
00:10:09,210 --> 00:10:09,930
It's not happening.

195
00:10:10,270 --> 00:10:11,110
And that's a big deal.

196
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:18,060
If you use put off when you actually meant call off, you're setting an expectation for something in the future that's never going to occur.

197
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:18,720
Exactly.

198
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:20,820
Imagine doing that with a client meeting.

199
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:23,800
That could be a costly mistake in terms of relationship management.

200
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:24,640
Definitely.

201
00:10:24,810 --> 00:10:30,340
Okay, and sticking with communication, particularly on the phone, that little particle on is crueful.

202
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:30,760
Hold on.

203
00:10:30,940 --> 00:10:31,940
Yeah, the classic phrase.

204
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:33,640
It just signals a short wait.

205
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:37,330
If a client calls, you might say, could you please hold on while I check that for you?

206
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:38,550
It's professional.

207
00:10:38,610 --> 00:10:39,130
It's brief.

208
00:10:39,150 --> 00:10:39,370
Right.

209
00:10:39,730 --> 00:10:46,840
And then for attendance or lack thereof, we track who arrived using turn up or show up examples.

210
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:48,710
He didn't turn up to the meeting yesterday.

211
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:50,650
He didn't show up to the scheduled meeting.

212
00:10:51,210 --> 00:10:52,590
Are they interchangeable?

213
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:53,100
Pretty much.

214
00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:55,440
Both mean to arrive or appear.

215
00:10:56,730 --> 00:11:01,850
But they were most often used when you're noting an absence, like in the examples, or perhaps a surprise arrival.

216
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:03,250
You know, guess who turned up?

217
00:11:03,650 --> 00:11:03,890
Right.

218
00:11:04,050 --> 00:11:10,580
If a key person doesn't show up or doesn't turn up for a meeting, that immediately signals a potential problem or disruption.

219
00:11:11,820 --> 00:11:15,930
The particle up here just denotes arrival at a specific place or event.

220
00:11:16,110 --> 00:11:16,450
Okay.

221
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:19,460
Finally, let's talk about concluding meetings.

222
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,500
We need to wrap things up professionally.

223
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:24,700
The verb often used is wind up.

224
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:26,110
Example.

225
00:11:26,670 --> 00:11:29,370
Let's wind up the meeting and summarize the key points.

226
00:11:29,750 --> 00:11:29,910
Yeah.

227
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:35,920
Wind up is a pretty standard, often efficient way to signal you're concluding or finishing an activity.

228
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:39,120
It very often signals that summary phase is about to begin.

229
00:11:39,180 --> 00:11:40,230
Efficient.

230
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:40,690
Okay.

231
00:11:41,270 --> 00:11:42,490
Is it ever too informal?

232
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:44,120
It can be, slightly.

233
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:50,270
For the most formal written conclusions, perhaps in official minutes, you might still opt for conclude or terminate.

234
00:11:50,810 --> 00:11:55,540
But in the flow of actually ending a meeting, wind up is very common and generally fine.

235
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:56,000
Gotcha.

236
00:11:56,460 --> 00:12:01,370
And related to time, something we often worry about, meetings going too long, we use run over.

237
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:04,350
Example, we ran over the main points at the end of the presentation.

238
00:12:04,750 --> 00:12:07,410
But hang on, does that mean going over time or reviewing points?

239
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:08,420
Ah, good catch.

240
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:10,820
It can actually mean both, which can be a bit confusing.

241
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:11,280
Right.

242
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:13,120
It very commonly means to exceed a time limit.

243
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:14,270
Sorry, everyone.

244
00:12:14,310 --> 00:12:16,190
The Q&A session ran over by 15 minutes.

245
00:12:16,210 --> 00:12:16,810
That's one meaning.

246
00:12:16,950 --> 00:12:17,250
OK.

247
00:12:17,430 --> 00:12:18,150
Exceeded time.

248
00:12:18,510 --> 00:12:27,590
But as in the example you gave, it can also mean to briefly explain or reiterate key points again, usually just before the final conclusion, like a quick review.

249
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:29,150
So context is key there.

250
00:12:29,410 --> 00:12:29,890
Absolutely.

251
00:12:30,010 --> 00:12:30,470
Yeah.

252
00:12:30,490 --> 00:12:32,880
But when you pair it with wind up.

253
00:12:32,930 --> 00:12:35,700
Let's wind up, but quickly run over the action items again.

254
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:45,350
It shows that even if maybe the discussion did exceed the allotted time slightly, the person leading the meeting ensured a proper, concise summary was still done.

255
00:12:45,450 --> 00:12:45,770
Right.

256
00:12:45,870 --> 00:12:46,150
Okay.

257
00:12:46,170 --> 00:12:56,290
This whole segment really highlights how phrasal verbs are just essential tools for controlling the flow of information, managing time, and just navigating those daily interactions in the workplace.

258
00:12:56,350 --> 00:12:57,450
Couldn't have said it better myself.

259
00:12:57,710 --> 00:12:59,070
They manage the meeting dynamics.

260
00:12:59,190 --> 00:12:59,430
Okay.

261
00:12:59,470 --> 00:12:59,880
Moving on.

262
00:13:00,170 --> 00:13:02,080
This next segment shifts focus a bit.

263
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:05,890
We're moving from the more tactical day-to-day stuff to the bigger picture.

264
00:13:05,870 --> 00:13:10,710
personal advancement, career paths, and those larger corporate structural shifts.

265
00:13:10,730 --> 00:13:11,150
Exactly.

266
00:13:11,550 --> 00:13:18,820
These verbs deal with professional ambition, leadership, and the sometimes difficult dynamics of authority and change within an organization.

267
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:26,330
And these are critical for anyone really focused on, you know, long-term professional success, navigating their career.

268
00:13:26,390 --> 00:13:26,870
Definitely.

269
00:13:27,290 --> 00:13:28,920
Let's start with ambition itself.

270
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:29,780
Get ahead.

271
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:35,800
The example given is, he worked hard to get ahead in his field by taking on extra projects.

272
00:13:36,170 --> 00:13:40,340
That directional particle ahead, it immediately signals progress, doesn't it?

273
00:13:40,420 --> 00:13:41,140
And competition.

274
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:42,780
You're not just working diligently.

275
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:48,970
You're actively trying to, well, outbase others, make measurable progress in your career or your specific field.

276
00:13:49,130 --> 00:13:49,610
Right.

277
00:13:49,630 --> 00:13:53,710
It often implies that initiative we talked about back in segment one with take on.

278
00:13:54,210 --> 00:13:57,660
You need to do extra things like taking on projects to actively get ahead.

279
00:13:57,820 --> 00:13:58,570
Makes sense.

280
00:13:58,590 --> 00:14:03,570
And as people progress, we inevitably find people we admire, mentors maybe, we look up to them.

281
00:14:03,770 --> 00:14:07,340
Example, many employees look up to their supervisor for guidance and inspiration.

282
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:10,350
Yeah, this is the language of professional admiration, of respect.

283
00:14:10,830 --> 00:14:13,930
It really highlights the importance of mentorship and positive leadership.

284
00:14:14,290 --> 00:14:17,600
So what does the up to part tell us here, linguistically speaking?

285
00:14:17,660 --> 00:14:18,180
Why up?

286
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:22,390
Well, the up particle usually implies a vertical difference in status or esteem.

287
00:14:23,010 --> 00:14:29,960
You're looking up towards someone who is higher on the ladder, either professionally or maybe even morally in your estimation.

288
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:33,410
It confirms that hierarchy of influence and respect.

289
00:14:34,030 --> 00:14:41,340
It's actually one of the most powerful phrasal verbs for describing a really healthy professional relationship that's based on inspiration and guidance.

290
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:42,120
Nice.

291
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:45,990
Now let's talk about transitions of authority.

292
00:14:46,610 --> 00:14:50,010
These can often be, well, a bit tense, even when they're planned.

293
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:55,580
The most common phrase for assuming control seems to be take over or sometimes take it over.

294
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:59,870
Example, the new manager will take over next month and implement changes.

295
00:15:00,310 --> 00:15:01,530
Yeah, this is pretty definitive.

296
00:15:01,590 --> 00:15:05,820
To take over means to assume control or responsibility, usually from a predecessor.

297
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:07,800
It marks a formal shift of power.

298
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:08,160
Right.

299
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:12,030
And it often signals that the incoming person will now start implement their own vision.

300
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:15,360
often initiating changes, like in the example.

301
00:15:16,020 --> 00:15:18,570
It conveys decisiveness and the arrival of new authority.

302
00:15:19,610 --> 00:15:19,710
Okay.

303
00:15:19,730 --> 00:15:25,440
And conversely, for leaving a position of authority, we have two distinct verbs, step down and step aside.

304
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:26,380
Examples.

305
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:29,300
The CEO decided to step down after 10 years.

306
00:15:29,850 --> 00:15:33,010
He decided to step aside and let younger leaders take the helm.

307
00:15:33,410 --> 00:15:34,350
What's the difference there?

308
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:36,420
It's subtle, but important.

309
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:43,190
Step down uses down quite literally signifying moving to a lower level or off the stage of authority.

310
00:15:43,750 --> 00:15:48,740
It means resigning or leaving a position voluntarily, often after a significant period, like a CEO example.

311
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:52,020
It's the general term for resignation from a high position.

312
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:54,480
OK, so step down is the standard resignation.

313
00:15:54,530 --> 00:15:55,170
Generally, yes.

314
00:15:55,770 --> 00:16:01,340
Step aside, however, that often carries an added nuance of making room specifically for others.

315
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:12,340
It implies a strategic move, possibly even a selfless one, relinquishing power, perhaps for succession planning or to deliberately usher in new leadership, like letting younger leaders take the helm.

316
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:15,560
You're literally moving aside to clear the path for someone else.

317
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:21,770
Understanding that slight difference is really key when you're interpreting, say, corporate announcements or news headlines.

318
00:16:21,950 --> 00:16:24,140
Yeah, that's a really useful distinction.

319
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:28,160
Now, these transitions often happen during bigger organizational changes.

320
00:16:28,790 --> 00:16:34,320
And unfortunately, we probably have to discuss one of the hardest verbs in the corporate dictionary, layoff.

321
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:35,440
Example.

322
00:16:35,910 --> 00:16:39,970
The company had to lay off some staff members last quarter to reduce expenses.

323
00:16:40,110 --> 00:16:42,960
Yes, and the language here needs to be really precise.

324
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:53,210
To lay off specifically means dismissing employees due to economic reasons, things like downsizing, restructuring, a downturn in business.

325
00:16:53,810 --> 00:16:57,600
Crucially, it is not the same as firing someone for poor performance.

326
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:57,920
Right.

327
00:16:57,960 --> 00:16:59,360
That's a critical difference.

328
00:16:59,380 --> 00:16:59,900
Absolutely.

329
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:06,110
Using layoff signals that it's an organizational shift, usually necessitated by financial pressures or market changes.

330
00:17:06,910 --> 00:17:11,220
It frames it as a corporate decision, not a reflection on the individual's performance or competence.

331
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:12,050
Okay.

332
00:17:12,070 --> 00:17:19,500
And to manage those kinds of shifts, especially if there are gaps in expertise needed for the change, organizations often need to bring in outside help.

333
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:22,460
Example, they brought in a consultant to help with the transition.

334
00:17:22,680 --> 00:17:22,870
Right.

335
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:27,750
That particle in clearly signals introducing something external into the organization.

336
00:17:27,830 --> 00:17:28,570
Yeah, from outside.

337
00:17:29,070 --> 00:17:29,510
Exactly.

338
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:35,420
It means recruiting someone, often temporarily, for their specific expertise or assistance.

339
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:45,880
This is the exact language you'd use when you need to fill a temporary skills gap or maybe inject some specialized, perhaps unbiased insight into a challenging situation like a transition.

340
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:53,780
And finally, in this segment, a verb that feels rooted in governance, maybe even politics, run for example.

341
00:17:54,570 --> 00:17:57,220
He decided to run for the position of team leader.

342
00:17:57,430 --> 00:18:01,720
Yeah, it definitely connects to that idea of a democratic or competitive process, even within a company.

343
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:05,240
It's the language of campaigning, of seeking office or a position.

344
00:18:05,340 --> 00:18:05,540
Right.

345
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:12,810
It signals a formal attempt to gain a specific role, and it usually implies needing to actively get support, maybe compete against other candidates.

346
00:18:13,430 --> 00:18:24,530
So this whole segment really shows how those directional particles, up, down, over, in, ahead, aside, they act like literal maps, charting movement within the professional hierarchy and organizational structure.

347
00:18:24,630 --> 00:18:25,210
Absolutely.

348
00:18:25,410 --> 00:18:26,490
Really paints the picture.

349
00:18:26,680 --> 00:18:30,160
All right, moving into arguably one of the most critical areas.

350
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:38,530
Business success, you know, it's often defined not just by avoiding problems, but by how effectively you handle them when they inevitably arise.

351
00:18:38,690 --> 00:18:38,910
Right.

352
00:18:39,350 --> 00:18:40,610
Managing the bumps in the road.

353
00:18:40,810 --> 00:18:41,320
Exactly.

354
00:18:41,670 --> 00:18:48,560
So this segment gives us the essential vocabulary for diagnosing issues, figuring out solutions, and recovering from setbacks.

355
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:50,470
It's the language of troubleshooting.

356
00:18:50,770 --> 00:18:52,730
Okay, let's start with the investigation phase.

357
00:18:53,070 --> 00:18:53,950
An issue comes up,

358
00:18:54,170 --> 00:18:55,230
A client complains.

359
00:18:55,550 --> 00:18:57,240
The first step is usually to look into it.

360
00:18:57,540 --> 00:18:57,880
Example.

361
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:02,000
The team will look into the client's concerns and provide a response soon.

362
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:02,360
Yes.

363
00:19:02,980 --> 00:19:06,970
Look into is just the absolute gold standard for professional investigation.

364
00:19:07,550 --> 00:19:10,330
It means researching a situation or an issue thoroughly.

365
00:19:10,930 --> 00:19:16,160
You're digging deeper to understand the cause, the context, before you jump to a solution or a response.

366
00:19:16,380 --> 00:19:17,500
So it signals thoroughness.

367
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:18,220
Absolutely.

368
00:19:18,240 --> 00:19:19,610
It shows a commitment to due diligence.

369
00:19:20,290 --> 00:19:27,400
That particle into really implies deep penetration, a careful examination beneath the surface, much stronger than just look at.

370
00:19:27,430 --> 00:19:27,750
OK.

371
00:19:28,210 --> 00:19:30,930
So once the investigation is done, you understand the problem.

372
00:19:31,830 --> 00:19:33,250
You need that strategic breakthrough.

373
00:19:33,350 --> 00:19:34,530
You need to figure out the puzzle.

374
00:19:35,210 --> 00:19:39,760
Example, we need to figure out how to reduce costs without sacrificing quality.

375
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:40,180
Right.

376
00:19:40,260 --> 00:19:42,720
This is the cognitive part, the problem solving phase.

377
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:50,170
Figure out is all about understanding or solving something complex, usually after some careful thought, maybe some analysis.

378
00:19:50,630 --> 00:19:54,730
It's that strategic moment where your analysis turns into a potential plan or solution.

379
00:19:54,880 --> 00:19:58,230
And then comes the practical resolution, maybe dealing with the mess.

380
00:19:58,290 --> 00:19:59,610
That's sort out example.

381
00:19:59,810 --> 00:20:03,090
We'll sort out the paperwork this afternoon to avoid delays.

382
00:20:03,110 --> 00:20:05,420
Yeah, I really like contrasting figure out and sort out.

383
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:09,060
Figure out is more the intellectual heavy lifting, the strategizing.

384
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:13,010
Sort out is more about the action of resolving the tangible issue.

385
00:20:13,330 --> 00:20:17,350
organizing the mess, putting things back in order, fixing the practical problem.

386
00:20:17,430 --> 00:20:18,490
Can you give another example?

387
00:20:18,650 --> 00:20:18,830
Sure.

388
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:24,320
Like if the main server goes down, you first need to figure out the root cause of the failure.

389
00:20:24,340 --> 00:20:25,120
That's the analysis.

390
00:20:25,740 --> 00:20:30,350
But while it's down, you might need to sort out a temporary system for manual data entry.

391
00:20:30,870 --> 00:20:32,970
That's the practical organizational fix.

392
00:20:33,020 --> 00:20:34,300
Okay, that clarifies it.

393
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:35,650
Figure out the why.

394
00:20:35,850 --> 00:20:36,970
Sort out the what now.

395
00:20:37,270 --> 00:20:37,670
Pretty much.

396
00:20:38,390 --> 00:20:40,380
All right, let's talk about unexpected things happening.

397
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:43,300
They never seem to announce themselves politely.

398
00:20:43,340 --> 00:20:44,990
First, we have come up.

399
00:20:45,830 --> 00:20:46,230
Example.

400
00:20:47,370 --> 00:20:50,760
A new opportunity has come up in the marketing department that might interest you.

401
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:54,550
Yeah, that particle up here signals sudden emergence.

402
00:20:54,570 --> 00:20:55,710
Something just appears.

403
00:20:56,270 --> 00:20:58,500
It means to appear or arise unexpectedly.

404
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:07,090
And it can be positive, like the opportunity in the example, or it could be neutral or even negative, like an unexpected issue that comes up mid-meeting and needs addressing.

405
00:21:07,070 --> 00:21:07,510
Okay.

406
00:21:08,050 --> 00:21:10,540
Conversely, we might unexpectedly encounter problems.

407
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:11,760
We might run into them.

408
00:21:12,180 --> 00:21:15,900
Example, we ran into some issues with the new software that need aggressing.

409
00:21:16,020 --> 00:21:16,240
Right.

410
00:21:16,580 --> 00:21:21,010
Run into means encountering or experiencing something suddenly, often a problem.

411
00:21:21,490 --> 00:21:27,070
Like hitting a roadblock, finding a technical glitch, maybe a conceptual difficulty you didn't anticipate.

412
00:21:27,530 --> 00:21:31,260
It implies a direct, usually accidental, collision with an obstacle.

413
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:35,060
Okay, so how does running into an issue compare to the really dreaded one?

414
00:21:35,550 --> 00:21:35,950
Breakdown.

415
00:21:36,550 --> 00:21:36,870
Example.

416
00:21:37,420 --> 00:21:39,210
The server broke down during the presentation.

417
00:21:39,410 --> 00:21:41,510
Ah, that's a crucial distinction.

418
00:21:42,310 --> 00:21:47,860
When you run into an issue, you're usually still functional, but maybe slowed down or facing a hurdle.

419
00:21:47,980 --> 00:21:48,160
Right.

420
00:21:48,240 --> 00:21:48,820
An obstacle.

421
00:21:49,020 --> 00:21:49,380
Exactly.

422
00:21:49,720 --> 00:21:52,750
But when something breaks down, it stops functioning properly.

423
00:21:52,910 --> 00:22:01,580
It implies a more complete systemic failure, like the server example, or maybe a negotiation that breaks down or a communication channel that completely fails.

424
00:22:01,780 --> 00:22:03,640
So it's a cessation of function.

425
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:09,830
Yeah, the concept of separating into smaller, non-functional pieces is kind of captured by that down particle.

426
00:22:10,270 --> 00:22:21,840
But remember, and this is important, breakdown also has that critical positive business meaning we touched on earlier to analyze something complex by breaking it down into smaller, understandable parts.

427
00:22:22,020 --> 00:22:22,280
Right.

428
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:23,540
The analytical meaning.

429
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:24,560
Good point.

430
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:25,960
OK, moving towards recovery.

431
00:22:26,110 --> 00:22:29,650
If we've fallen behind schedule or missed something, we need to catch up.

432
00:22:29,670 --> 00:22:30,270
Example.

433
00:22:30,400 --> 00:22:33,610
I'll catch up with you after I finish this task to discuss the project status.

434
00:22:34,050 --> 00:22:36,510
Yes, essential for project management, teamwork.

435
00:22:37,350 --> 00:22:44,320
It means reaching the same level as others who might be ahead, or maybe updating someone on progress after a period of absence or delay.

436
00:22:44,860 --> 00:22:48,450
It always implies needing some focused effort to close that gap.

437
00:22:48,590 --> 00:22:52,710
And to compensate for that delay or that gap, we might need to make up for lost ground.

438
00:22:53,050 --> 00:22:57,220
Example, we need to make up for lost time on this project to meet deadlines.

439
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:57,800
Exactly.

440
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:01,170
Makeup is the language of compensation, of remediation.

441
00:23:01,650 --> 00:23:06,140
It signifies rectifying a deficiency or offsetting something that was missed.

442
00:23:06,160 --> 00:23:12,660
If you miss a deadline, you have to find ways to make up the lost time, maybe working longer hours, maybe allocating more resources.

443
00:23:12,640 --> 00:23:12,940
Right.

444
00:23:13,020 --> 00:23:13,940
Fixing the deficit.

445
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:18,110
Now, here's the really high stakes recovery verb turnaround example.

446
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:21,350
The new strategy helped turn around the company's declining sales.

447
00:23:21,410 --> 00:23:21,670
Oh, yeah.

448
00:23:21,690 --> 00:23:22,340
This one's big.

449
00:23:22,760 --> 00:23:27,200
That directional particle around indicates a complete reversal, like a 180 degree shift.

450
00:23:27,680 --> 00:23:29,010
This isn't just a slight improvement.

451
00:23:29,290 --> 00:23:31,050
This signals a monumental change.

452
00:23:31,410 --> 00:23:37,300
It's often used in the context of a financial rescue, pulling something back from a failing situation.

453
00:23:37,530 --> 00:23:45,950
A credible professional uses turnaround when they're talking about major significant positive reversals in key metrics or performance.

454
00:23:46,730 --> 00:23:50,680
The kind of change that might save a whole business line or even the entire company.

455
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:54,570
It's really the language of like organizational heroism sometimes.

456
00:23:54,890 --> 00:23:55,030
Wow.

457
00:23:55,310 --> 00:23:55,790
OK.

458
00:23:55,770 --> 00:24:11,580
So this whole segment really makes it clear that being able to articulate that entire problem-solving process from looking into the initial issue to figuring out the right solution and then hopefully being able to turn around a bad situation that's absolutely fundamental to effective management, isn't it?

459
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:12,420
Couldn't agree more.

460
00:24:12,540 --> 00:24:15,700
It's how you demonstrate control and competence when things go wrong.

461
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:16,950
All right, our final segment.

462
00:24:17,330 --> 00:24:22,820
This brings us back to perhaps the human element and also the need for continuous effort.

463
00:24:22,840 --> 00:24:30,700
It focuses on things like networking, persistence, knowing your boundaries, and just keeping up with the relentless pace of modern business life.

464
00:24:30,720 --> 00:24:33,090
Again, all expressed through these concise phrasal verbs.

465
00:24:33,340 --> 00:24:33,700
Okay.

466
00:24:34,340 --> 00:24:36,540
Let's start with networking and connection.

467
00:24:36,580 --> 00:24:41,370
There's the dual use of get in touch and keep in touch example.

468
00:24:41,650 --> 00:24:44,120
Let's keep in touch even after the project is over.

469
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:44,580
Right.

470
00:24:44,780 --> 00:24:50,150
So get in touch is usually about making that initial contact or reestablishing contact after a while.

471
00:24:50,210 --> 00:24:50,530
Okay.

472
00:24:50,610 --> 00:24:51,690
Initiate contact.

473
00:24:51,730 --> 00:24:51,840
Yeah.

474
00:24:51,820 --> 00:24:55,720
And keep in touch is about the maintenance, the ongoing communication over time.

475
00:24:56,020 --> 00:25:00,070
And that maintenance is just crucial for long term career success, isn't it?

476
00:25:00,090 --> 00:25:03,930
It's how you build and importantly, sustain your professional network.

477
00:25:04,190 --> 00:25:04,630
Definitely.

478
00:25:04,930 --> 00:25:10,520
And the quality of those day to day relationships within a team that seems summed up by get along with.

479
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:14,450
Example, she gets along with all her colleagues, making teamwork easier.

480
00:25:14,430 --> 00:25:19,320
Yeah, this describes having a harmonious or at least a friendly functional relationship.

481
00:25:19,340 --> 00:25:22,480
It's really the foundation of, you know, psychological safety within a team.

482
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:25,310
You don't necessarily have to be best friends with everyone.

483
00:25:25,350 --> 00:25:25,610
Right.

484
00:25:26,030 --> 00:25:31,800
But you absolutely must be able to get along with your colleagues to make collaboration smooth and effective.

485
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:32,500
Makes sense.

486
00:25:33,220 --> 00:25:40,190
And when we're anticipating future connections or events positively, we often express eagerness using look forward to.

487
00:25:40,450 --> 00:25:43,060
Example, I'm looking forward to the upcoming conference.

488
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:46,560
Yes, this shows enthusiasm, positive anticipation.

489
00:25:46,700 --> 00:25:54,490
It's a very standard professional and polite way to express genuine interest in a future activity, a meeting, a collaboration.

490
00:25:55,150 --> 00:25:55,910
Adds a nice touch.

491
00:25:56,140 --> 00:26:02,380
OK, now let's shift to the language of effort and persistence, especially in a market that's constantly changing.

492
00:26:03,220 --> 00:26:06,900
To survive, maybe even thrive, you have to keep up with it all.

493
00:26:08,210 --> 00:26:11,430
it's challenging to keep up with the latest industry trends and developments.

494
00:26:11,530 --> 00:26:14,760
That up with combination really emphasizes matching pace, doesn't it?

495
00:26:14,780 --> 00:26:15,720
Yeah, staying level.

496
00:26:15,940 --> 00:26:16,400
Exactly.

497
00:26:16,580 --> 00:26:25,630
It means staying informed, constantly learning, maintaining parity with whatever dynamic environment you're in, whether that's changing technology, new competitors, shifting regulations.

498
00:26:26,010 --> 00:26:26,730
You have to keep up.

499
00:26:26,930 --> 00:26:30,740
And when maybe results aren't good enough or there's a crisis, you might need to step up your game.

500
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:34,910
Example, we need to step up our customer service efforts to retain clients.

501
00:26:34,990 --> 00:26:35,250
Right.

502
00:26:35,790 --> 00:26:41,800
The directional particle up here clearly signals an increase or an improvement in effort or performance.

503
00:26:41,840 --> 00:26:42,760
Helping the intensity.

504
00:26:42,900 --> 00:26:43,460
Precisely.

505
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:43,900
Yeah.

506
00:26:43,920 --> 00:26:51,450
Raising the level of commitment or quality, especially when maybe performance has been lagging or when a situation demands a new level of urgency.

507
00:26:52,070 --> 00:26:54,690
It's really a call to immediate heightened action.

508
00:26:54,960 --> 00:26:55,440
Okay.

509
00:26:55,460 --> 00:26:59,960
And then there's just that simple grinded out determination that seems captured by keep at.

510
00:27:00,360 --> 00:27:02,990
Example, if you keep at it, you'll eventually see results.

511
00:27:03,100 --> 00:27:08,010
Yeah, keep that really emphasizes persistence, especially when things are difficult or tedious.

512
00:27:08,630 --> 00:27:16,210
If you're wrestling with a complex financial model or dealing with a particularly difficult client, you just have to keep at the task.

513
00:27:16,870 --> 00:27:20,080
It's a real testament to sustained, focused effort.

514
00:27:20,620 --> 00:27:27,340
That particle almost suggests being kind of stuck to or focused intently on a single point of effort, refusing to let go.

515
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:28,680
Right, dogged persistence.

516
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:33,110
Okay, the final group here deals with setting professional boundaries and managing tolerance levels.

517
00:27:33,090 --> 00:27:35,170
Starting with give up that one seems pretty clear.

518
00:27:35,770 --> 00:27:37,110
Just stop trying.

519
00:27:37,270 --> 00:27:38,030
Abandon the effort.

520
00:27:38,050 --> 00:27:38,250
Right.

521
00:27:38,290 --> 00:27:38,890
Straightforward.

522
00:27:38,910 --> 00:27:40,600
But what about the more nuanced one?

523
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:41,580
Put up with.

524
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:42,620
Example.

525
00:27:42,900 --> 00:27:43,000
Yeah.

526
00:27:43,320 --> 00:27:45,360
I can't put up with this level of stress any longer.

527
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:48,220
Ah, this is crucial for well-being and setting boundaries.

528
00:27:48,770 --> 00:27:53,950
It means to tolerate or endure something unpleasant or challenging, often over a period of time.

529
00:27:54,050 --> 00:27:54,350
Okay.

530
00:27:54,510 --> 00:27:54,930
Endure.

531
00:27:55,130 --> 00:27:55,410
Yes.

532
00:27:56,130 --> 00:27:58,780
And when an employee or even a manager says,

533
00:27:58,760 --> 00:28:03,700
states clearly that they can't put up with a situation anymore, like the stress level in the example.

534
00:28:04,340 --> 00:28:08,790
They are very clearly signaling that the limit of their professional tolerance has been reached.

535
00:28:09,670 --> 00:28:15,050
This often necessitates a change, either in their environment, the process, or maybe even their role.

536
00:28:15,260 --> 00:28:16,140
It's a strong statement.

537
00:28:16,220 --> 00:28:16,820
It really is.

538
00:28:16,860 --> 00:28:22,580
And speaking of boundaries and decisions, professionals often find themselves having to turn down offers or requests.

539
00:28:22,900 --> 00:28:26,370
Example, she had to turn down the job offer because it required relocating.

540
00:28:26,350 --> 00:28:31,280
Yeah, to turn down is simply to reject or refuse something, an offer, a request, an invitation.

541
00:28:31,380 --> 00:28:32,560
It's decisive.

542
00:28:32,580 --> 00:28:38,590
Linguistically, that phrase, turn down, kind of implies deflecting something away from you or denying it entry.

543
00:28:38,870 --> 00:28:40,660
And think about the weight of that decision.

544
00:28:41,260 --> 00:28:46,870
In the example, rejecting a whole job offer distilled into just two concise words.

545
00:28:46,850 --> 00:28:51,760
Delivering that rejection, of course, requires professional diplomacy to do it cleanly and respectfully.

546
00:28:51,940 --> 00:28:52,440
Absolutely.

547
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:53,380
Okay.

548
00:28:53,400 --> 00:28:54,610
And finally, vigilance.

549
00:28:55,050 --> 00:28:57,370
So important for managing risk.

550
00:28:57,390 --> 00:29:02,260
Look out for, example, make sure to look out for any potential risks during the project.

551
00:29:02,310 --> 00:29:02,570
Yes.

552
00:29:02,730 --> 00:29:09,800
This means being attentive, being vigilant, being proactively aware in anticipation of potential problems or opportunities.

553
00:29:10,240 --> 00:29:14,810
It's a core directive in any kind of project management, safety protocol or security context.

554
00:29:14,850 --> 00:29:15,670
Scanning the horizon.

555
00:29:15,910 --> 00:29:16,390
Exactly.

556
00:29:16,410 --> 00:29:20,200
You're proactively scanning the horizon for issues before they hit you.

557
00:29:20,950 --> 00:29:32,670
So, yeah, this final segment really shows how these phrasal verbs let us articulate complex things like relationship dynamics, emotional endurance, setting boundaries and career persistence, all with really succinct accuracy.

558
00:29:32,850 --> 00:29:36,040
It's amazing how much meaning is packed into these little combinations.

559
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:37,480
Hashtag, tag, tag, outro.

560
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:41,210
So if we try and, you know, connect this all back to the bigger picture.

561
00:29:42,470 --> 00:29:44,990
We've really covered a huge amount of ground, haven't we?

562
00:29:45,490 --> 00:29:51,020
Traversed pretty much the entire landscape of professional life just using these linguistic building blocks.

563
00:29:51,050 --> 00:29:51,970
Yeah, feels like it.

564
00:29:52,150 --> 00:29:57,920
We've seen how these simple verb particle combinations let us signal initiation when we set up meetings.

565
00:29:58,380 --> 00:29:58,720
Right.

566
00:29:58,740 --> 00:30:03,140
How we convey resilience by deciding to carry on through difficulties.

567
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:16,620
how we navigate those big organizational shifts when, say, a CEO steps down and even how we articulate that massive positive change when a really bad project or situation is finally turned around.

568
00:30:16,940 --> 00:30:24,570
It really does provide a comprehensive, high impact toolkit, doesn't it, for anyone trying to navigate today's global professional environment effectively?

569
00:30:24,790 --> 00:30:25,230
I think so.

570
00:30:25,590 --> 00:30:31,380
And the key takeaway for you, the listener, is that these aren't just, you know, vocabulary items to memorize for a test.

571
00:30:31,490 --> 00:30:32,680
They are active tools.

572
00:30:32,660 --> 00:30:39,210
tools for execution, tools for subtle social signaling, tools for conveying commitment and understanding.

573
00:30:39,610 --> 00:30:49,120
Using them correctly and confidently is really key to sounding natural, sounding authoritative, and just seeming truly well informed in any business context.

574
00:30:49,700 --> 00:30:59,120
When you can confidently say you'll look into a client's complaint or that you'll keep it a difficult task until it's done, that projects real competence.

575
00:30:59,100 --> 00:30:59,800
Absolutely.

576
00:31:00,300 --> 00:31:09,090
And maybe as a final provocative thought for you to consider as you go about your week, we mentioned earlier that dual meaning of the phrasal verb bring down.

577
00:31:09,160 --> 00:31:10,980
Right, the analysis part and the failure part.

578
00:31:11,080 --> 00:31:11,620
Exactly.

579
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:19,990
So one meaning is analysis, taking something complex and breaking it down to make it understandable, which is basically what we've just spent this whole time doing with this topic, right?

580
00:31:20,010 --> 00:31:20,650
Yeah, meta.

581
00:31:21,350 --> 00:31:21,630
Sort of.

582
00:31:22,300 --> 00:31:31,990
And we've also seen verbs that do the inverse, like put together, which means assembling various elements into a cohesive whole, like putting together a report or putting together a new strategic plan.

583
00:31:32,370 --> 00:31:33,410
Ah, I see where you're going with this.

584
00:31:33,630 --> 00:31:34,590
Break down and put together.

585
00:31:34,930 --> 00:31:37,380
Think about those two concepts, that pairing.

586
00:31:37,490 --> 00:31:48,630
The ability to analytically break down a problem, really identify its core components, and then the skill to strategically put together the resulting solution or plan.

587
00:31:48,850 --> 00:31:57,320
That cycle, that pairing, it almost summarizes the entire analytical and constructive process that's required for so much professional success.

588
00:31:57,300 --> 00:32:05,560
So maybe as you engage with your tasks this week, try consciously applying that pair, break down and put together to how you approach things.

589
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:10,740
You might find the very structure of professional thinking becomes that little bit clearer, a bit more organized.

590
00:32:10,990 --> 00:32:12,110
That's a fantastic connection.

591
00:32:12,210 --> 00:32:13,450
Break it down, put it together.

592
00:32:13,470 --> 00:32:15,690
A great way to think about applying these tools.

593
00:32:15,730 --> 00:32:16,580
Thanks for that deep dive.

594
00:32:16,710 --> 00:32:17,080
My pleasure.

595
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:18,180
Okay.

596
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:22,400
This is all about signaling responsibility and execution power.

597
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:26,510
The first verb you really need to signal proactivity is take on.

598
00:32:26,710 --> 00:32:27,190
Take on.

599
00:32:27,710 --> 00:32:32,120
This means deciding to accept a new responsibility, a challenge, a task.

600
00:32:32,140 --> 00:32:36,080
It's often used when an employee volunteers for something, maybe a stretch goal.

601
00:32:36,350 --> 00:32:39,830
Feels strategic because take-on implies initiative, doesn't it?

602
00:32:40,190 --> 00:32:44,140
Whereas simply accepting a project sounds, well, passive.

603
00:32:44,360 --> 00:32:45,280
It can, yes.

604
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:49,740
A manager is much more likely to remember the employee who took on that difficult client account.

605
00:32:50,070 --> 00:32:50,570
Precisely.

606
00:32:51,010 --> 00:32:53,210
So once you've taken on the task, you need some structure.

607
00:32:53,370 --> 00:32:54,350
That's where setup comes in.

608
00:32:54,530 --> 00:32:55,290
Setup, okay.

609
00:32:55,310 --> 00:33:05,020
It implies arranging, preparing, or establishing something crucial, like setting up the parameters for a new software system or setting up a key strategic planning session, but laying the groundwork.

610
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:05,320
Right.

611
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:06,140
Getting things ready.

612
00:33:06,710 --> 00:33:10,070
And following that groundwork, the mandate has to be fulfilled.

613
00:33:10,370 --> 00:33:13,860
We move from planning to execution with carryout.

614
00:33:14,480 --> 00:33:14,860
Oh, yes.

615
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:15,300
Carryout.

616
00:33:15,400 --> 00:33:17,020
I find this one particularly strong.

617
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:21,510
The sources emphasize that carrying out a plan means executing it as intended.

618
00:33:21,570 --> 00:33:26,130
It really conveys a sense of adherence, fidelity to the proposal.

619
00:33:26,110 --> 00:33:33,190
That strong, almost, well, military style execution route is why it's so powerful in formal proposals.

620
00:33:33,610 --> 00:33:36,250
The team will carry out the project exactly as outlined.

621
00:33:36,330 --> 00:33:37,440
That gives confidence.

622
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:44,410
Now, for the administrative side of execution, sometimes you need to delegate or maybe cover for someone temporarily.

623
00:33:44,710 --> 00:33:45,590
That's look after.

624
00:33:45,570 --> 00:33:46,470
Okay, look after.

625
00:33:46,490 --> 00:33:47,980
I'm interested in the distinction here.

626
00:33:48,140 --> 00:33:52,770
If I say she will handle the client accounts, that sounds like a formal transfer of duties.

627
00:33:52,890 --> 00:33:53,430
It does, yeah.

628
00:33:53,670 --> 00:33:58,520
But if I say she'll look after the client accounts while the manager is away, what does the phrasal verb add?

629
00:33:58,540 --> 00:33:59,220
What's the nuance?

630
00:33:59,740 --> 00:34:03,330
It adds a sense of temporary stewardship.

631
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:04,350
Care.

632
00:34:05,090 --> 00:34:09,900
As if the person is safeguarding the assets, not formally taking ownership.

633
00:34:09,920 --> 00:34:15,030
It's often used for more informal delegation or assistance when the primary person is absent.

634
00:34:15,050 --> 00:34:17,150
It signals, I'm keeping an eye on this for you.

635
00:34:17,450 --> 00:34:18,360
Ah, okay.

636
00:34:18,380 --> 00:34:20,340
Less about ownership, more about temporary care.

637
00:34:21,200 --> 00:34:25,250
And wrapping up this initiation phase related to personal growth, we have take-up.

638
00:34:25,230 --> 00:34:25,770
Take up.

639
00:34:25,790 --> 00:34:25,990
Yes.

640
00:34:26,010 --> 00:34:31,520
This is the decision to start doing something regularly, especially something that improves your skills.

641
00:34:31,580 --> 00:34:31,840
Right.

642
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:35,590
You might take up additional duties to gain experience in a new department.

643
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:43,250
That's a fantastic way to position personal ambition, using a phrase that sounds purposeful rather than just, you know, volunteering for extra work.

644
00:34:43,370 --> 00:34:43,810
Purposeful.

645
00:34:44,030 --> 00:34:44,470
I like that.

646
00:34:44,590 --> 00:34:47,530
OK, that covers the language of initiation and responsibility.

647
00:34:47,910 --> 00:34:50,740
But so much of business function relies on communication.

648
00:34:51,040 --> 00:34:51,460
Absolutely.

649
00:34:51,640 --> 00:34:57,550
Let's shift focus now to the dynamics of meetings, reporting, scheduling, all that good stuff.

650
00:34:57,530 --> 00:34:57,810
Right.

651
00:34:58,210 --> 00:35:03,270
So when you move into a discussion, you need to know how to introduce a topic without just saying, let's talk about.

652
00:35:03,590 --> 00:35:05,650
You bring up the topic or raise an issue.

653
00:35:06,010 --> 00:35:06,410
Bring up.

654
00:35:06,740 --> 00:35:11,960
For instance, bringing up the sales figures during the meeting to discuss maybe poor performance.

655
00:35:12,620 --> 00:35:17,070
It implies the information was previously held back or perhaps unaddressed.

656
00:35:17,090 --> 00:35:20,870
OK. And once that information is on the table, the flow continues.

657
00:35:20,910 --> 00:35:22,810
You need to relay messages efficiently.

658
00:35:22,830 --> 00:35:24,070
That's pass on.

659
00:35:24,290 --> 00:35:25,170
Simple direct.

660
00:35:25,530 --> 00:35:28,580
Please pass on the new policy update to the rest of the department.

661
00:35:28,820 --> 00:35:29,260
Concise.

662
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:30,300
Very clear.

663
00:35:30,400 --> 00:35:30,960
Yeah.

664
00:35:30,980 --> 00:35:32,700
And, oh, the administrative side.

665
00:35:33,090 --> 00:35:34,150
It never sleeps, does it?

666
00:35:34,390 --> 00:35:35,250
Unfortunately not.

667
00:35:35,530 --> 00:35:38,200
The unavoidable completion of mandatory documents.

668
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:40,180
That's handled by fill-out.

669
00:35:40,200 --> 00:35:43,080
You must fill out the expense report before finance can process it.

670
00:35:43,180 --> 00:35:44,750
The bane of many in existence, yes.

671
00:35:45,190 --> 00:35:45,590
Fill-out.

672
00:35:45,770 --> 00:35:48,600
Now let's talk about the universal challenge, time management.

673
00:35:49,060 --> 00:35:51,300
We constantly face delays, cancellations.

674
00:35:51,620 --> 00:35:55,750
What's the subtle difference between pushing something back and eliminating it entirely?

675
00:35:55,910 --> 00:35:58,020
We have a couple of verbs for this.

676
00:35:58,060 --> 00:35:58,420
We do.

677
00:35:58,460 --> 00:36:03,170
If you are delaying or postponing an event, you put off the meeting until next week.

678
00:36:03,650 --> 00:36:05,730
It implies a known future date, usually.

679
00:36:05,850 --> 00:36:06,730
Put off.

680
00:36:06,710 --> 00:36:07,430
Postpone.

681
00:36:07,510 --> 00:36:12,600
But if you were outright canceling a planned event or action, that's call off.

682
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:15,430
They called off the contract negotiations after the merger failed.

683
00:36:15,830 --> 00:36:16,710
It's finished.

684
00:36:17,190 --> 00:36:17,410
Done.

685
00:36:17,610 --> 00:36:18,330
Call off.

686
00:36:18,470 --> 00:36:18,750
Final.

687
00:36:19,270 --> 00:36:24,220
And we noted in the source material that two verbs often confused relate to obstruction or waiting.

688
00:36:24,780 --> 00:36:26,500
There's the brief internal pause.

689
00:36:26,680 --> 00:36:27,920
Hold on like on the phone.

690
00:36:27,960 --> 00:36:30,130
Please hold on while I retrieve that file.

691
00:36:30,210 --> 00:36:30,410
Yes.

692
00:36:30,830 --> 00:36:32,370
Short duration, usually voluntary.

693
00:36:32,850 --> 00:36:35,470
And then there is it's much more frustrating sibling.

694
00:36:35,730 --> 00:36:36,210
Hold up.

695
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:37,560
Ah, hold up.

696
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:42,780
This conveys being delayed or having progress obstructed, usually by an external factor.

697
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:46,620
If the budget review was held up by the legal department, let's see the difference.

698
00:36:46,960 --> 00:36:49,050
That subtle separation of agency is key.

699
00:36:49,430 --> 00:36:50,170
We were held up.

700
00:36:50,410 --> 00:36:51,870
We didn't just put it off ourselves.

701
00:36:51,920 --> 00:36:54,470
That distinction regarding agency is critical, isn't it?

702
00:36:54,830 --> 00:36:58,490
If our own team causes the delay, maybe we ran over the time limit.

703
00:36:58,630 --> 00:36:58,810
Right.

704
00:36:58,970 --> 00:37:02,500
But if the customs inspection caused the delay, the shipment was held up.

705
00:37:02,520 --> 00:37:05,910
One implies internal accountability, the other external obstruction.

706
00:37:06,010 --> 00:37:06,450
Exactly.

707
00:37:06,790 --> 00:37:10,790
And speaking of accountability, we need verbs for attendance and status checks.

708
00:37:11,350 --> 00:37:13,380
We use turn up or show up for arriving.

709
00:37:13,520 --> 00:37:14,740
Turn up or show up.

710
00:37:14,870 --> 00:37:18,140
And the professional context often highlights the negative, doesn't it?

711
00:37:18,720 --> 00:37:20,740
She didn't turn up for the final pitch.

712
00:37:20,760 --> 00:37:23,120
It emphasizes a critical failure of attendance.

713
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:24,980
Yeah, that definitely sounds negative.

714
00:37:25,100 --> 00:37:29,830
And when you are actively managing tasks, you need to check in or check in with.

715
00:37:29,930 --> 00:37:33,490
I find check in with particularly useful professionally.

716
00:37:33,510 --> 00:37:33,750
Me too.

717
00:37:33,930 --> 00:37:34,510
It's good.

718
00:37:34,530 --> 00:37:37,880
It implies a quick sort of non-disruptive update loop.

719
00:37:37,900 --> 00:37:38,340
Absolutely.

720
00:37:38,760 --> 00:37:42,900
I'll check in with you next week to see how the software integration is progressing.

721
00:37:42,880 --> 00:37:47,170
It signals active monitoring without demanding a formal status meeting.

722
00:37:47,750 --> 00:37:50,040
It respects the time of the person doing the work.

723
00:37:50,240 --> 00:37:50,500
Right.

724
00:37:50,680 --> 00:37:51,460
It's lighter touch.

725
00:37:51,980 --> 00:37:53,640
Okay, moving on from communication flow.

726
00:37:53,840 --> 00:37:59,810
Let's talk about analysis and problem solving the core function of, let's be honest, highly paid professionals.

727
00:37:59,910 --> 00:38:00,430
Indeed.

728
00:38:01,090 --> 00:38:05,320
When dealing with a report or a situation, you have two key approaches for examination.

729
00:38:06,020 --> 00:38:12,090
First, if you are reviewing something carefully to ensure accuracy and completeness, you go over the report.

730
00:38:12,780 --> 00:38:14,050
Think quality control.

731
00:38:14,210 --> 00:38:14,710
Go over.

732
00:38:14,910 --> 00:38:15,810
Okay, the first pass.

733
00:38:16,150 --> 00:38:19,370
And if you need to dig deeper because maybe a fault was found during the go-over?

734
00:38:19,430 --> 00:38:20,150
Exactly.

735
